 "intro": "Nola in Campania, late 216 BC, a few months after Cannae. Much of southern Italy has gone over to Hannibal. You are the praetor Marcus Claudius Marcellus, holding the town, whose common people want to join him.",
 "debrief": "In Livy’s account, Marcellus held Nola’s gates with Roman troops, and when Hannibal approached expecting the town to betray itself, he burst out from three gates at once and drove him back. It was a small victory, and Livy says the numbers were exaggerated, but it was the first time since Cannae that Hannibal had been checked, and it raised Roman spirits. About seventy plotters in the town were executed. Hannibal attacked Nola again in the following two years without success. The other choices are what-ifs.",
